+# Immersive Investigation Redesign Plan
+
+**Specification package; no runtime or Drive changes.**
+
+## Scope
+Write and review 45 encounter specifications, 15 complete location packages, shared interaction/state rules, dependency records and contributor tasks. Publish the reviewed files to this game's main branch. Google Drive remains unchanged. Existing source content, saves, workflows and runtime mechanics are protected.
+
+## Work sequence
+1. Recover the pinned game and inventory actual clues, items, choices, APIs and source prose.
+2. Write concrete encounter actions and reconcile proposal conflicts before treating them as source-compatible design.
+3. Define pressure, accessible input, logical progress and safe restore.
+4. Define asset requests and exact implementation boundaries; distinguish installed APIs from prerequisite tasks.
+5. Trace complete and alternate journeys, validate all links/coverage, record five sequential review passes and publish.
+
+## Future implementation sequence
+Complete CAP-INPUT and CAP-MECHANISM contracts, then CAP-PROJECTION, CAP-SAVE and CAP-SEQUENCE proof. Implement Town Gate from this package and review actual player understanding. Continue through Centre/Café/Market, Apothecary/Tower, School/Archive/Chapel, Parish/Tunnels/Well and the three resolution rooms. Every group must preserve source outputs before the next migrates.
+
+## Definition of done for this delivery
+All encounter files contain actual clue/action/response/completion specifications. All source required clues remain obtainable without optional lore. Each room has flow, asset, implementation and acceptance files. Every task has an ID, input, boundary, dependencies and observable acceptance. Five pass records retain exact revision deltas. Links, source mappings and generated files validate. Runtime completion is not claimed.
+
+## Risks still requiring future work
+Unfamiliar-player understanding, actual duration, final models and animation, performed voices, physical input devices, target hardware and save migration behavior require implementation and direct evidence. No document approval makes those results true.
